like this is gonna really transform your  space you know there's so many things to  do with this feature you know it can be  an armature for hosting indigenous  artwork so today  i am just gonna ramble i  i just wanted to share something that  happened to me at work  a couple of weeks ago that was a little  bit of a wake-up call for me  and it kind of forced me to check my own  attitude  and reflect on what being an architect  means for me  hey guys welcome back to the channel if  you're  new here my name is danny and i'm a  licensed architect  in beautiful vancouver bc on this  channel we talk about  architecture and design and also some  tools and strategies that can help us  have more meaningful  and fulfilling careers in architecture  by the way  i shared this story a couple weeks ago  in my bi-weekly newsletter  when it was all just raw and fresh  and now i want to tell you that story  again  verbally because i've had some time to  reflect  and think about it by the way if you are  interested in joining my newsletter  i will leave the link below i've been  getting some  really positive feedback although i've  only put out  one newsletter so far i would love it if  you guys  want to join so right now i'm working on  a  renovation of a library uh you you might  have noticed that libraries right now  are going through  a massive transformation with  more and more books becoming digitized  and  with more and more people absorbing  content  online libraries are turning more into  what we're calling a community hub where  they host events  and workshops and so we're working with  a group of librarians and  and were taking some of the original  functions of the building  a lot of which was used to store  and sort books and were turning them  into  public spaces like a digital media space  and a lounge  and and we're also fixing some of the  functional problems  of the building unfortunately the  project is confidential at the moment so  i  i can't share any images of the project  but soon  so the existing building it's a  three-story building  with a massive three-story atrium  at the entrance which is  problematic in a lot of ways and one of  the problems  is that there's very little visual  coherence  there's like little bits of windows and  there's like the existing travertine  tile that like  runs in different angles and it's just  like  a bit of a mess and it's a real missed  opportunity  because it could really be a special  place like this grand entrance  and secondly there's also  huge problems with acoustics there's um  there's like sound transfer and  reverberation so  when you're sitting on the second floor  you can kind of hear  everything that's happening on the first  floor i was there when a  kid was throwing a temper tantrum and it  was like  really really really disruptive for the  entire library  the librarians they um  wanted to solve some of these issues but  they also wanted this entrance to be  like a celebration of this neighborhood  and this community which is really  diverse  and they also really wanted to program  the space  for functions because right now it's  just used for circulation  and so one of our proposals was  an acoustic feature that would hang in  the center of the atrium  so first of all it would act as an  acoustic buffer  but at the same time you could project  onto it you could project down from it  onto the floor  and it would create lots of different  possibilities for using this atrium and  really allow us to program  the atrium for different events  um it would also tie together some of  the  like random visual elements in the space  um so yeah we were super excited about  this idea  and when we first presented it to the  librarians they were  super excited um and so we were like  yes and so we started designing uh  designing the this feature but in our  next subsequent uh feedback session  they were like um yeah so we thought  about it  we consulted the rest of our staff and  we decided we don't want the atrium  feature  we have come to the conclusion that we  want to  leave the atrium completely empty  for future artwork by indigenous artists  so i was  pretty bummed out i was like for real  you do realize that this thing's gonna  be really cool right this is  um yeah in the end it was still  it was a no it was no  um so i was pretty bummed out  and just that week we  we heard the news that they found  the remains of 215  indigenous children buried  under unmarked graves in a  residential school in kamloops and  actually just this week they found more  bodies they found something like  700 bodies in a residential school  in saskatchewan for those of you who are  not familiar with the history of canada  basically western settlers moved here  they stole land and they  committed horrific humanitarian crimes  against indigenous people  and like residential schools were a  really big part of that  of that cultural cleansing they were  like these mandatory boarding schools  funded by the canadian government and  operated by the christian church  and they were designed to assimilate  indigenous children  into the canadian culture  and these were really horrible horrible  horrible places  these kids were physically and sexually  abused  they were ripped apart from their  families and their culture and their  language  and they were forced to speak english  and french the worst part is that  they ran for more than a hundred years  there's still a lot of people saying  you know that's in the past it was  a hundred like distant years ago  this canada the canada that we live in  now  it means so much more but actually it's  not  so much in the past the last residential  school closed  not more than 30 years ago if you listen  to my interview with scott kemp  i'll leave the link here he's an  architect who works mostly with  indigenous communities he talked about  how the communities that he's worked  with  are still being like systemically  discriminated against still today  there's also this thing called  intergenerational trauma which means  if your parents or grandparents were  survivors of like years and years of  racism  of physical and sexual abuse of having  no rights  then it's very very highly likely that  those traumas are gonna be passed down  onto you  well that's the definition of  intergenerational trauma but  in this case i'm actually like not  talking about  trauma that's been passed down like my  friend jake she just shared a posting  that  she still has friends and family who are  like direct survivors of the residential  school system who have been  directly affected by this  for me like in the midst of my  everyday existence in my day-to-day job  of being an architect  um like design buildings put up the  walls  i guess i had kind of neatly  stacked canada's history into a corner  as something to be dealt with or  something that i have to work with  and um it was just a reminder for me  that like as long as i am a person  living and benefiting from this  beautiful city  i also have a responsibility to  um like at least reflect on this  and as long as i'm an architect working  in this unseated territory i  have a responsibility to um like spend  more time  learning about the city's reconciliation  efforts and  how i can contribute to that there's a  really great quote by  jane jacobs where she says the first  fundamental  of successful city life people must take  a modicum of responsibility for each  other even if  they have no ties to each other this is  a lesson no one learns  by being told it is learned from the  experience  of having other people without ties or  kinship or close friendship  or formal responsibility to you take  a modicum of responsibility for you  life can get very insular you know i was  joking with my friends the other day  that  um like especially as you get older your  your  circle of concern the people that you  spend  time with and the people that you spend  energy on  it gets tighter and tighter and you know  it's  it's not really your fault and like  modern society  kind of conditions us to be like this  and it kind of conditions us to  be like me me me me me me i love jane  jacobs because  so much of what she writes about and  what she fights for  is just like creating an environment for  basic  human decency and basic human  connections  like the building blocks of a good city  is an environment where  when you see someone in need whether you  know them or not  you help them not because you're the  kindest person ever but that's just  because it's something that people do  and um when you're in need and when  something  happens to you you can trust that other  people in your community are going to  help you  so if a group of people in your  community  has experienced these like horrific  immense injustices in their lives  i think we need to kind of try our best  to bring them up  and you know give them a voice and like  make this space  feel like they are a part of it  and that they were a part of designing  it  and no i am not so arrogant as to say  that  like as to think that architecture or  urban planning  can solve these like very complex social  economic or cultural issues but i think  at least like we could do our best to  elevate the vulnerable people as we  design our environment and even if it's  just through  the integration of artwork like we can  do that in a meaningful way  um i think artwork can play a role  because  like we're putting it in front of the  public's eyes like big  and proud as a constant reminder of  like in this case who's whose land we're  on  and hopefully like we can celebrate the  indigenous community in  um in some way for me  uh moving forward i'm gonna try to learn  more about  local indigenous artists talk to them  like learn about their work learn what  it means  and um think about how i can integrate  their work  into into the designs  i feel like a lot of times architects  say  oh maybe we can integrate public art  here  but we never really do that with any  specificity  and i'm totally guilty of this um  and like we design with a very high  level of specificity with  everything else so why is this any  different  right um i also learned from the city  recently that the city is trying to  integrate public art in in the very  early stages of the building's design so  as we're designing the building um  we can work with the artists and maybe  the artwork  can inform the building and also vice  versa  and i think it's the process that's  really important  i think as architects especially as  architects um in the 21st century  we we need to be a little bit more than  just designers  as much as i'd love to just design cool  beautiful things  we kind of have to solve a lot of the  problems of our  past generation so we kind of need to  get  more involved in the community need to  learn about the culture  and the history of the place that we  live in  i actually mentioned in my newsletter  too but i think some of the most  successful architects  that i've seen are generous and they're  caring  and they're very attentive to  um all the little things that are  happening in their communities  and i think that can also be kind of a  competitive advantage  okay guys that's it for today thanks for  watching  uh let me know what you think in the  comments below  do you think that you have a  responsibility to your community  thanks for listening to my ramble all  right bye see you in the next video  [Music]  you

